1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Getting a reply back from Claude is only half the job.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Read it the wrong way and your code breaks the moment a tool call or a thinking block shows up first.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This lesson covers the response object, how to read its blocks, the stop reason field, and usage counts. Let's dig in.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Every response Claude sends back has three parts worth knowing by name.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The first is content, the list of blocks the model returned.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
You loop over that list and handle each block by its type.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Rather than reaching for the first one and hoping.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The second is stop reason, a single field that names why the model stopped this turn.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Your code branches on it to decide what to do next.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The third is usage, the token accounting for the call.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
It reports how much input and output the request consumed, which is exactly what you watch for cost.

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Read those three every time.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Content tells you what Claude said, stop reason tells you what to do, and usage tells you what it cost.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Here's the habit that keeps response handling from breaking.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The content field is a list, so you read it by looping over it, never by grabbing a fixed index.

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
As you loop, you branch on each block's type field and handle it accordingly.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
This matters because the order isn't guaranteed.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A thinking block or a tool use block can easily come before the text you were expecting.

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
So if you reach straight for the first block and assume it's text, your code will work in testing.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
And then break in production the first time something else lands in front.

21
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Handle text, tool calls, and thinking each in their own branch.

22
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And the same handler works no matter what shape the reply takes.

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Let's walk through four of the most common stop reason values.

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
An intern means Claude finished on its own, so you take the text and you're done.

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:59,000
A max tokens means the reply hit your output cap, so raise the cap or continue it in a follow-up request.

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
A tool use means Claude paused to ask for a tool, so you run it and return the result.

27
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And a refusal means Claude declined on safety grounds.

28
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
The single case where stop details gets filled in.

29
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
For every other value, it stays empty.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Three more round out the roster.

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Stop sequence when a custom stop string fires.

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Model context window exceeded when the response hits the model's total window.

33
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And pause turn when a long turn pauses.

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
So you send the assistant content back to continue.

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
A loop that only waits for end turn can silently break on pause turn.

36
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Let's slow down on the tool use case because it's a small loop you'll write over and over.

37
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
When stop reason comes back as tool use, Claude is asking your code to do something.

38
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
First, you read the tool use block to get the tool's name and the input it wants.

39
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Second, you run that tool inside your own application.

40
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Nothing runs on Anthropic's side here.

41
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Third, you append a tool result block carrying the output and call the API again with the updated conversation.

42
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Then Claude picks up right where it left off.

43
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:19,000
That cycle repeats as many times as needed, and it ends when stop reason finally comes back as end turn instead.

44
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Read, run, append, recall.

45
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
That's the whole pattern.

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Let's put the two most common stop reasons side by side because your loop pivots on the difference.

47
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
When stop reason is end turn, the model is finished.

48
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
You read the final content, stop the loop, and hand the answer back to whoever asked.

49
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
There's nothing more for you to do.

50
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
When stop reason is tool use, the model has paused and handed control to you.

51
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:54,000
You execute the requested tool, append the result as a block, and recall so Claude can keep going.

52
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The key mental model is this.

53
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
End turn is an exit, and tool use is a yield.

54
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
One ends the conversation, the other suspends it and waits on your code.

55
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
If you can tell those two apart instantly, the rest of the response loop falls into place naturally.

56
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Let's read the usage object because it's how you track what a call actually costs.

57
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
The first field is input tokens.

58
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
This counts the uncached input tokens the call billed, meaning only the remainder that wasn't served from the cache.

59
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
That's a common surprise, so remember input tokens isn't your whole prompt.

60
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
The second is output tokens, every token the model generated in its reply.

61
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
That's the part your max token's ceiling limits.

62
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Then come the cache fields, separate counts for tokens written to the cache and tokens read from it.

63
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Here's the practical rule.

64
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
To get the full prompt size, you add all three input figures together.

65
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
The uncached input plus both cache counts.

66
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Watch these numbers and cost stops being a mystery.

67
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Here's a small guard that saves you from a crash.

68
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
There's a field called stop details that carries extra context about why the model stopped, but it's

69
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
only populated in one situation.

70
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
That situation is a refusal when stop reason comes back as refusal on Opus Form 7.

71
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
And later, stop details fills in with a category and an explanation of what was declined.

72
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:28,000
For every other stop reason, whether that's intern or tool use or max tokens, stop details comes back null.

73
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
The order of your checks matters.

74
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:36,000
If you reach for stop details on a normal reply, you're reading a null value and risking an error.

75
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Always check stop reason first, confirm it's a refusal, and only then read stop details.

76
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Guard first, read second, and this never bites you.

77
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:54,000
One more rule keeps multi-turn conversations from falling apart, and it's about how you carry blocks forward.

78
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Thinking blocks first, the extended reasoning blocks you'll meet properly in the thinking lesson.

79
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
When a reply includes thinking, you pass those blocks back unmodified on the latest assistant turn,

80
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
keeping the signature and everything else exactly intact.

81
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Editing them, even slightly, gets the call rejected.

82
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Tool blocks work the same way.

83
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:21,000
You carry the tool use and tool result blocks forward as they were, without trimming or reformatting.

84
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
If you drop them or change them, the very next call fails because the model can't reconcile the conversation.

85
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
So the discipline is simple.

86
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Whatever structured blocks came back on the latest assistant turn, you send them back untouched.

87
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Treat those blocks as read-only, and your multi-turn flow stays stable.

88
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Here's the bottom line.

89
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Reading a response comes down to looping over content by type, branching on stop reason, and

90
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,000
checking usage for cost.

91
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:56,000
It matters because indexing blindly or misreading the stop reason is where response handlers quietly break down.

92
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Just remember, pass thinking and tool blocks back unchanged, and guard stop details behind a refusal check.

93
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
That wraps up reading stop reason and usage.

94
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
I'll see you in the next one.

